This would greatly alleviate human stress, but be careful when swapping the company,children or animals.

To transform it into an iron human, the robot is too crude in its movements on its own, but it's very mature in terms of hints.

An robot offline, and ecologic is out of the question; it could be a robot connected by satellite, behind a super server, and it seems to me that someone is concerned about doing this off-Earth.

It's a long way from replacing a brain, since I believe more in DNA for this purpose.

But an AI injected into the brain to make decisions paths, would have a amazing result.

A hybrid brain with AI on a chip, for example, could provide instant answers and complex reasoning in seconds, potentially raising the bar to a higher level.

I think he'll get bored with so much brainpower.

Now imagine when he masters powers, sixth senses, my god, instantaneous things like magic.

I think I watched a lot of Dragon Ball as a child, but we'll get pretty close to that.

It seems like I see that part of the episode where the Cell Fictional character is floating across the plains, cutting the plains through them and creating a wide and plain arena with hand gestures.

Perhaps new discoveries, powers, mutants, bio-bots, xenobots, neurobots—it's a blank notebook.


Connecting a robot to an AI via satellite internet only requires a Wi-Fi connection to the server, accessing the REST API, and translating audio, for example.

Look, AI is excellent for conversation and creating structures, but when it comes to mixing code and structures, trying to solve specific problems within all of that, sometimes it freezes code in loops, resulting in many errors and bugs that seem like a snowball effect, as it keeps guessing until one works.

Passed another someday, I started a new conversation to resolve a problem, and it was done in 15 minutes. We finished, and I saved the link. Later, I opened the link and tried to resolve it another way. An hour and a half passed, and I saw that it wasn't going to be resolved and was only getting worse. I was already lost with the direction the conversation was taking. I was frustrated knowing that I was entering a point of no return and that it wasn't going to be resolved.

Years ago, a Google search would give you links to the most visited topics related to that keyword. Today, AI does the hard work that would take you minutes, hours, or even days, depending on the keyword and topic. AI gives you answers in seconds; it's not an absolute answer, just an answer.


Just as the result is 4, there are several ways to obtain this result, such as 2+2, 3+1, 5-1, etc.

She understands, but she can't provide a single, definitive solution. She has human knowledge but doesn't know how to solve it; would that lose its charm?

For each question, she tries a different approach.

Why is there no absolute answer, or does she not know how to give an absolute answer?

The result is only one, but there are several ways to do it, and that's where she gets stuck.

If you have knowledge about what you're going to ask, the sky's the limit, but if you don't know or it's unknown, your experience will be determined by how she interprets it.


In a final conversation that lasted almost three whole days, I decided to really test it out, and look what happened...

